window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"][13]={"university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M13-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M13-L1 – Automation Structures and Sequential Control<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M13 – Bachelor: Automation, Control and Robotics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Finite state\nmachines<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Control\nloops<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Hybrid\nmodels<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Break down an automation task\ninto discrete operating states, continuous control loops and verifiable\ntransition conditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Levels of an Automated Plant<\/h2>\n<p>An industrial plant must do more than stabilise a physical quantity.\nIt must also decide when a subprocess is enabled, which setpoint applies\nand how to handle a fault. An automated positioning drive, for example,\nconsists of a position sensor, a drive controller, a motor with power\nelectronics and a supervisory sequential controller. The drive\ncontroller generates a manipulated variable from the desired and actual\npositions; the sequential controller determines when the drive\ncontroller may request movement in the first place. A user interface\ndisplays the operating state, commands and diagnostics. It need not be\npart of the fast control loop.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level<\/th>\n<th>Example task<\/th>\n<th>Typical signal in the teaching model<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Field level<\/td>\n<td>Measurement and actuation<\/td>\n<td>Angle, speed, torque<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feedback control<\/td>\n<td>Limit the deviation<\/td>\n<td>Setpoint, commanded torque<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sequential control<\/td>\n<td>Determine steps and enables<\/td>\n<td>State, start, done<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Supervision<\/td>\n<td>Logging and operation<\/td>\n<td>Fault code, event time<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>These tasks may have different execution periods. In the example, the\naxis controller is called every 5 ms, the sequential controller every 20\nms and the display every 200 ms. These times are purely design\nassumptions, not product specifications. A fast sensor is of little use\nif an outdated value is passed to the controller unnoticed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sequential Control of a Positioning Station<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_042_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Four operating states with normal transitions and a prioritised fault path.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The state machine separates motion, target reached\nand fault handling; faults or timeouts take priority and lead to a fault\nstate requiring controlled acknowledgement.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The station starts in READY (<code>BEREIT<\/code>). With a valid\nenable signal and a new rising start edge, it changes to MOVING\n(<code>FAHREN<\/code>); the target angle is stored when the command is\naccepted. After reaching the tolerance window (position and velocity\nthresholds), it enters DONE (<code>FERTIG<\/code>). Acknowledgement\nreturns it to READY. A fault or missing enable signal takes priority\nover normal transitions. A timeout is also monitored in MOVING. The\nFAULT state (<code>FEHLER<\/code>) is latched: a fault can only be\nacknowledged once the enable signal is valid again and the cause of the\nfault has disappeared. A new start edge must occur separately.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>State<\/th>\n<th>Action in the abstract model<\/th>\n<th>Normal transition<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>READY<\/td>\n<td>No motion command<\/td>\n<td>Start edge → MOVING<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MOVING<\/td>\n<td>Output the target trajectory<\/td>\n<td>Target reached → DONE<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DONE<\/td>\n<td>Hold position<\/td>\n<td>Acknowledge edge → READY<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FAULT<\/td>\n<td>Motion command inhibited<\/td>\n<td>Enable valid, cause resolved, acknowledge edge → READY<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>This is a <strong>functional teaching model<\/strong>, not a safety\ncontroller. Inhibiting a motion command guarantees neither standstill\nnor the holding of a vertical load. For a real plant, risks, braking\ndistance, stored energy and appropriate independent safety functions\nmust be considered separately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Cyclic Execution and Priority<\/h2>\n<p>In a simplified cyclic controller, input data are acquired at the\nstart of a cycle, the logic is evaluated and output data are updated. A\nshort pulse appearing between two samples can go undetected. Input\nfilters, buffered events or edge-capture hardware are possible\ncountermeasures; these themselves change the timing conditions.\nSequential Function Chart (SFC) according to IEC 61131-3 provides steps\nand transitions for structuring sequences; our Python example represents\nonly a deterministic state machine, not a standards-compliant PLC\nruntime. The original code retains the German state names listed\nabove.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"co\"># Zeitueberschreitung und Flanken werden vom aufrufenden Zeitmodell geliefert.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> step(s, ready<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>, fault<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">False<\/span>, timeout<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">False<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>         start<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">False<\/span>, done<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">False<\/span>, ack<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">False<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> fault <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> ready <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> (s <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;FAHREN&#39;<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> timeout):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;FEHLER&#39;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> s <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;FEHLER&#39;<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;BEREIT&#39;<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> ack <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> s<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> s <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;BEREIT&#39;<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> start:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;FAHREN&#39;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> s <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;FAHREN&#39;<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> done:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;FERTIG&#39;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> s <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;FERTIG&#39;<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> ack:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;BEREIT&#39;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> s<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>s <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> step(<span class=\"st\">&#39;BEREIT&#39;<\/span>, start<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> s <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;FAHREN&#39;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> step(s, done<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>, fault<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;FEHLER&#39;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> step(s, timeout<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;FEHLER&#39;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> step(<span class=\"st\">&#39;FEHLER&#39;<\/span>, ack<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>, ready<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">False<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;FEHLER&#39;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> step(<span class=\"st\">&#39;FEHLER&#39;<\/span>, start<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;FEHLER&#39;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> step(<span class=\"st\">&#39;FEHLER&#39;<\/span>, ack<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>, start<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;BEREIT&#39;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> step(step(s, done<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>), ack<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;BEREIT&#39;<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The simultaneous occurrence of a fault and the done signal is\ndeliberately tested. The order of the conditions is part of the\nspecification. For cyclic execution, the caller must detect edges using\nthe stored previous input, for example\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∧<\/mo><mo>¬<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_k\\land\\neg s_{k-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf start remains active, this prevents a second command from being\nissued upon acknowledgement. A timeout monitor counts from entry into\nMOVING; the counter is not reset every time that state is output\nagain.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Timing Budget and Test Design<\/h2>\n<p>For a cycle of 20 ms and an event that remains stable at the input,\nwaiting for the next sample alone can take almost 20 ms. Filtering,\nexecution, bus transmission and updating the output add further delays.\nWith an assumed filter delay of 3 ms, a logic budget of 4 ms and an\noutput transport delay of 5 ms, a simplified upper response bound of 32\nms results, provided no cycle is missed. For an axis cycle of 5 ms, this\nslow sequential controller must not be mistaken for the immediate speed\ncontrol loop.<\/p>\n<p>A test plan includes normal cases, boundary cases and timing faults:\ndouble clicks, a held start button, missing feedback, a fault coinciding\nwith target attainment, and acknowledgement without an enable signal. An\nobservation point must distinguish physical standstill from a logical\nstate indication.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A button is active for 8 ms between two samples, and the cycle lasts\n20 ms. Is detection guaranteed?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> No. The pulse can fall entirely between\nsamples.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In MOVING, <code>done<\/code> and <code>timeout<\/code> are true\nsimultaneously. Which state follows?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> FAULT, because monitoring has priority.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A sensor has frozen after a communication failure and displays the\ntarget angle. What additional check is necessary?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> Check measurement quality and age as well as\nvelocity; an outdated position value is not a valid done\nindication.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Digital\ncontrol and saturation<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.plcopen.org/standards/logic/iec-61131-3/status-iec-61131-3-standard/\">PLCopen\n– Status of IEC 61131-3 and SFC<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · The 4th Edition section:\ncontroller languages ST, LD, FBD and SFC structure; public overview, not\nfull standard. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.plcopen.org/downloads/\">PLCopen – Introductions\nand downloads on sequential control languages<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Downloads: SFC\ntextual (DE/EN), introduction to SFC (German); listing only, files not\nopened. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M13-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M13-L2 – Digital Control and Saturation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M13 – Bachelor: Automation, Control and Robotics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">z-transform<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Sampling<\/a>,\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Stability\nand an introduction to controller design<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Develop a discrete-time PI\ncontroller and quantitatively assess the effects of actuator saturation\nwith and without integrator limiting.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From the Continuous to the Sampled Model<\/h2>\n<p>A digital controller reads the measured value at discrete times\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_k=kT_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the simplest case, its control signal remains constant between\nupdates (zero-order hold). The delay and frequency response of this hold\nelement form part of the plant. An excessively long sampling period can\ndegrade an originally stable design; however, a short period alone does\nnot guarantee stability when computation and communication delays are\nalso present.<\/p>\n<p>We use a normalised first-order plant with time constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau\\dot y+y=u,\\qquad |u|\\le1.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The state solution for a constant held input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nproduces the exact discrete equation<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_{k+1}=ay_k+(1-a)u_k,\\qquad a=e^{-T_s/\\tau}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_s=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.90484<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\approx0.90484<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn Euler step would give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand would be an approximation. Here, the exact sampled model separates\nthe control effect from integration errors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>PI Controller and Windup<\/h2>\n<p>We store the integral contribution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndirectly in units of the manipulated variable. The setpoint is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the error is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_k=r_k-y_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_P=1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_I=3\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">clip<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_k=K_Pe_k+z_k,\\qquad u_k=\\operatorname{clip}(v_k,-1,1),\\qquad z_{k+1}=z_k+T_sK_Ie_k.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If the setpoint is 2 but the plant can only reach values up to 1, the\nerror remains positive. Without limiting the integral contribution,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontinues to grow. When the setpoint later becomes attainable again, the\nmanipulated variable often remains at its limit for quite some time.\nThis behaviour is called integrator windup.<\/p>\n<p>With conditional integration,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis updated only when the error does not reinforce saturation. At the\nupper limit, integration is disabled for a positive error; at the lower\nlimit it is disabled for a negative error. An alternative,\nback-calculation, adds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_sK_b(u_k-v_k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the integrator equation; the gain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas units of 1/s. Like other controller parameters, it must be tested\nrather than chosen arbitrarily large.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_043_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Setpoint change at two seconds: conditional integration shortens recovery after actuator saturation; the integral contributions and outputs of both controllers are shown.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Following the setpoint change, the unprotected\nintegrator remains wound up under actuator saturation; conditional\nintegration limits this windup and speeds recovery.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Stability without Saturation<\/h2>\n<p>For a constant setpoint, deviations from the equilibrium are\nconsidered. If the control signal is not limited and conditional\nintegration is inactive, the linear state model is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>δ<\/mi><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>δ<\/mi><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>a<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>δ<\/mi><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>δ<\/mi><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{bmatrix}\\delta y_{k+1}\\\\\\delta z_{k+1}\\end{bmatrix}=\n\\begin{bmatrix}a-(1-a)K_P&amp;1-a\\\\-T_sK_I&amp;1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\delta y_k\\\\\\delta z_k\\end{bmatrix}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At 50 ms, the eigenvalues are approximately 0.881 ± 0.011 i (both\nmagnitudes less than 1). This applies locally around an unsaturated\noperating point; it does not prove global stability of the switched\nsystem. Design variants must also be tested with load changes, actuator\nlimits and measurement errors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Numerical Experiment<\/h2>\n<p>The setpoint is initially 2 and changes to 0.5 after 2 s. The initial\nvalues are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=z=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe following Python code uses the exact plant solution.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> math<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> pi(T<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">0.05<\/span>, aw<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    y<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>z<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">0.0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>math.exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>T<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"fl\">0.5<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    out<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> k <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>T)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        t<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>k<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>T<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        r<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">2.0<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> t<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        e<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>r<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>y<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        v<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>e<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>z<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        u<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>(<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,v))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        out.append((t,r,y,u,z))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> aw <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> ((v<span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> e<span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> (v<span class=\"op\">&lt;-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> e<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            z<span class=\"op\">+=<\/span>T<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>e<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        y<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>a<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>y<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>a)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>u<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> out<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> T <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"fl\">0.05<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.025<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> aw <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"va\">False<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">True<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        d <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> pi(T, aw)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(v[<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]) <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> v <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> d)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(T, aw, d[<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>T)], d[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-20\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Sampling period<\/th>\n<th>Conditional integration<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Integral contribution at 2 s<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Output at 5 s<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">50 ms<\/td>\n<td>no<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7.54738<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.99995<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">50 ms<\/td>\n<td>yes<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.49692<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">25 ms<\/td>\n<td>no<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7.50965<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.99995<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">25 ms<\/td>\n<td>yes<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.49617<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>At 50 ms, the variant with conditional integration first comes within\n0.025 of the new setpoint at 2.20 s. This must not be equated with a\npermanent settling time. The variant without limiting does not reach the\nband at all by the end of the experiment at 5 s. Repeating the\nexperiment with half the sampling period confirms the qualitative\nbehaviour, although the numerical values vary slightly. Even with\nanti-windup, the controller cannot achieve the physically impossible\nfirst target; it simply recovers after returning to the feasible\nrange.<\/p>\n<p>In real systems, limits may apply not only to amplitude but also to\nactuator slew rate, current, temperature and voltage. If the effective\nmanipulated variable is subject to downstream limits, the anti-windup\nlogic must know about them. In a cascade controller, for example, the\nouter position loop may request a speed target that the inner loop\ncannot deliver.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe value of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat is stored under conditional integration?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{k+1}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbecause the error is positive and the upper limit has been reached.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=1.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith a negative error, is integration disabled?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> No. A negative contribution can bring the\nintegrator back out of upper saturation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is an eigenvalue magnitude below one insufficient for all\noperating conditions?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> The matrix describes the unsaturated linear\ncase. Switching logic, delays and other limits change the dynamics.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Robot\nkinematics and coordinate systems<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.mathworks.com/help/simulink/slref/discretepidcontroller.html\">MathWorks\n– Discrete PID Controller and anti-windup<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Anti-Windup Control\nUsing PID Controller Block example: saturation and integrator windup. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.mathworks.com/help/mcb/ref/picontroller.html\">MathWorks\n– PI Controller: Clamping and Back-calculation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Description\nsection: discrete PI/PID controller structure. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M13-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M13-L3 – Robot Kinematics and Coordinate Systems<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M13 – Bachelor: Automation, Control and Robotics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Vectors and\nsystems of linear equations<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Differential\nequations<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine the position and\nvelocity of a planar robot from its joint angles, distinguish inverse\nsolutions and recognise singularities.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Joint Space and Workspace<\/h2>\n<p>An articulated robot can reach the same tool point with different\njoint configurations. Joint space describes the independent drive\ncoordinates. The workspace denotes the reachable positions (and, for\nmore general robots, orientations). We restrict ourselves to a planar\nrobot with two ideal revolute joints and rigid links of lengths\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l_1=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nm and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l_2=0.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nm. The axes are perpendicular to the plane. Angles are positive\nanticlockwise and are expressed in radians in the equations.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis defined relative to the first link; the absolute orientation of the\nsecond link is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_1+q_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_044_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Annular ideal workspace and two joint configurations for the same tool point at x = 0.4 and y = 0.3 metres.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Within the annular workspace, two different joint\nconfigurations can reach the same tool position; selecting a branch is\npart of inverse kinematics.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The rotation of a planar coordinate follows from<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>q<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>q<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>q<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>q<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(q)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos q&amp;-\\sin q\\\\\\sin q&amp;\\cos q\\end{bmatrix}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The forward kinematics is the sum of the two rotated link\nvectors:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=l_1\\cos q_1+l_2\\cos(q_1+q_2),\\qquad\ny=l_1\\sin q_1+l_2\\sin(q_1+q_2).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Without joint limits or collisions, the geometric workspace is an\nannulus with radii\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|l_1-l_2|=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nm and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l_1+l_2=0.7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nm. A real robot cannot reach every position in this ideal region because\nof mechanical stops, obstacles and self-collisions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Inverse Kinematics<\/h2>\n<p>For a desired position\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x,y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe law of cosines gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>l<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>l<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">atan2<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_2=\\frac{x^2+y^2-l_1^2-l_2^2}{2l_1l_2},\\quad\nq_2=\\operatorname{atan2}(\\pm\\sqrt{1-c_2^2},c_2).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|c_2|&gt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthere is no real solution. Small numerical overshoots due to rounding\ncan be clipped to the interval\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[-1,1]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter checking a tolerance. A genuinely unreachable point, however, must\nnot be silently moved into the workspace. For each of the two possible\nsign choices, we determine<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">atan2<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">atan2<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_1=\\operatorname{atan2}(y,x)-\\operatorname{atan2}(l_2\\sin q_2,l_1+l_2\\cos q_2).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nm and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=0.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nm, we obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_2=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOne solution is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_1=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_2=\\pi/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe other is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">atan2<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.287<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_1=2\\operatorname{atan2}(0.3,0.4)\\approx1.287<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrad and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_2=-\\pi/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth lead to the same tool point but to different orientations and joint\nconfigurations. Along a continuous path, a feasible family of solutions\nis selected and followed as continuously as possible.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Differential Kinematics and Singularities<\/h2>\n<p>The time derivative of the position vector gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>J<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot p=J(q)\\dot q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Jacobian matrix contains the derivatives with respect to the joint\nangles:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=\\begin{bmatrix}\n-l_1\\sin q_1-l_2\\sin(q_1+q_2)&amp;-l_2\\sin(q_1+q_2)\\\\\nl_1\\cos q_1+l_2\\cos(q_1+q_2)&amp;l_2\\cos(q_1+q_2)\n\\end{bmatrix}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Its determinant is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l_1l_2\\sin q_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen the arm is fully extended or folded, the matrix is singular. In\nthese configurations, the tool point cannot be moved arbitrarily in two\nindependent Cartesian directions. Near a singularity, moderate tool\nvelocities may require very high joint velocities. A practical path\nplanner therefore checks not only reachability but also the conditioning\nof the mapping, joint limits and the required dynamics.<\/p>\n<p>At the point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=(0,\\pi/2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.3<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.3<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.3&amp;-0.3\\\\0.4&amp;0\\end{bmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.03<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.04<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot p=(0.03,0.04)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nm/s, we obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot q_1=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrad/s and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot q_2=-0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrad/s. The static force mapping is also relevant: equality of virtual\npower\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>M<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F^T\\dot p=M^T\\dot q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>J<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>F<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=J^TF<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nprovided that no other loads are considered. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=(10,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nN at this point, we obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=(-3,-3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nNm for the corresponding tool force.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Computational Experiment<\/h2>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> math <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> sin, cos, atan2, sqrt, pi, hypot<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>l1, l2 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.3<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> forward(q1, q2):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> (l1<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>cos(q1)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>l2<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>cos(q1<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>q2),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            l1<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>sin(q1)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>l2<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>sin(q1<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>q2))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> inverse(x, y):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    c <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (x<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>y<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>y<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>l1<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>l1<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>l2<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>l2)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>l1<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>l2)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(c) <span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-12<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;ausserhalb des Arbeitsraums&#39;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    c <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>(<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,c))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    sols <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> []<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> sign <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        q2 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> atan2(sign<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>c<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>c),c)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        q1 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> atan2(y,x)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>atan2(l2<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>sin(q2),l1<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>l2<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>cos(q2))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        sols.append((q1,q2))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> sols<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x,y <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> ((<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.3<\/span>),(<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>),(<span class=\"fl\">.7<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>),(<span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> q1,q2 <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> inverse(x,y):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        X,Y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> forward(q1,q2)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> hypot(X<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x,Y<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>y) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1e-10<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(x,y,q1,q2)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">try<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    inverse(<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">except<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">pass<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">else<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-30\"><a href=\"#cb1-30\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">AssertionError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;unerreichbarer Punkt akzeptiert&#39;<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The check substitutes the angles obtained from inverse kinematics\nback into the forward kinematics. It also detects a point outside the\nannulus. At its boundaries, the two solutions may coincide up to angle\nperiodicity. Passing a geometric test does not yet demonstrate that an\naxis controller can implement the trajectory.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nreachable?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> No, the minimum radius is 0.1 m.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_1=\\pi/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_2=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,0.7)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nm. The configuration is singular.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can a Cartesian straight line be followed without checking\nintermediate points if both endpoints are reachable?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> No. The straight line may pass through the\ninner unreachable region or through obstacles. Joint limits and\nvelocities must also be checked.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Trajectories\nand axis control<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://modernrobotics.northwestern.edu/chapters/chapter5/\">Lynch\nand Park – Modern Robotics, Chapter 5: Velocity Kinematics and\nStatics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 5: velocity kinematics; video index, videos not\nreviewed. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://modernrobotics.northwestern.edu/chapters/chapter6/\">Lynch\nand Park – Modern Robotics, Chapter 6: Inverse Kinematics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter\n6: inverse kinematics; video index, videos not reviewed. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M13-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M13-L4 – Trajectories and Axis Control<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M13 – Bachelor: Automation, Control and Robotics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Motor\noperating point<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Coupled\nmodels<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Robot\nkinematics<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Design a time-parameterised\njoint motion, account for axis dynamics and verify the achievable\npositioning accuracy using a simulation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Path and Trajectory<\/h2>\n<p>A path specifies the geometric sequence of configurations. A\ntrajectory additionally specifies when they are reached. In joint space,\nthe path can be specified as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(s)=q_A+s(q_B-q_A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le s\\le1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA straight-line connection in joint space does not generally yield a\nstraight line in Cartesian space. If the tool is to travel along a\nstraight line, the Cartesian path must be converted into joint\ncoordinates and checked for singularities (see <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L3<\/a>).<\/p>\n<p>For a single axis with an initial angle of zero and a target angle of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=1.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrad, we use\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns and the normalised time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=t/T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe cubic time function is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mi>s<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>Q<\/mi><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_r=Q(3s^2-2s^3),\\quad\n\\dot q_r=\\frac{Q}{T}(6s-6s^2),\\quad\n\\ddot q_r=\\frac{Q}{T^2}(6-12s).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Outside the motion interval, we hold the respective end positions\nwith zero velocity and acceleration. At both ends the velocity is\ncontinuous; acceleration has discontinuities. A quintic function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10s^3-15s^4+6s^5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncould also bring acceleration continuously to zero at the endpoints. For\ncubic motion, the peak values are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5<\/mn><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.5Q/T=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrad/s and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6Q/T^2=1.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrad/s². For limits\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_\\max<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_\\max<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe therefore require\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\ge1.5|Q|/v_\\max<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\ge\\sqrt{6|Q|/a_\\max}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis check concerns the reference trajectory; the actual system must be\nchecked separately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Axis Model, Feedforward and Feedback<\/h2>\n<p>We use an ideal rotary axis with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkg m² and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nNm s/rad. The electrical dynamics are represented by an ideal inner\ntorque controller. The model equations are<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>J<\/mi><mover><mi>ω<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot q=\\omega,\\qquad J\\dot\\omega=M-b\\omega.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A PD position controller compares the angle and velocity with the\nreference trajectory. With feedforward,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>J<\/mi><msub><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><msub><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">clip<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_v=J\\ddot q_r+b\\dot q_r+K_P(q_r-q)+K_D(\\dot q_r-\\omega),\n\\qquad M=\\operatorname{clip}(M_v,-2,2)\\,\\mathrm{Nm}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The saturation limits are numerical values in Nm;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_P=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nNm/rad and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_D=2.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nNm s/rad. Feedforward supplies the torque required for the ideal\nreference motion. Feedback compensates for deviations from the model.\nWithout feedforward, feedback must first detect an error before torque\nis generated. With an exact continuous-time model, no saturation and\ninstantaneous feedback, the error equation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mover><mi>e<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mover><mi>e<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/msub><mi>e<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J\\ddot e+(b+K_D)\\dot e+K_Pe=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe poles are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6.25<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mn>0.968<\/mn><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-6.25\\pm0.968i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n1/s; both real parts are negative. This conclusion does not directly\napply to delayed digital feedback.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reproducible Axis Simulation<\/h2>\n<p>For constant torque within a sampling step, the mechanical\ndifferential equation is integrated exactly. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=e^{-bT_s/J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_\\infty=M/b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>J<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nq_{k+1}&amp;=q_k+\\omega_\\infty T_s+(\\omega_k-\\omega_\\infty)(1-a)J/b,\\\\\n\\omega_{k+1}&amp;=\\omega_\\infty+(\\omega_k-\\omega_\\infty)a.\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The code already includes an optional measurement delay in whole\nsampling steps, which will be used in the next lesson. If no prior\nhistory is available, the initial state is used. Changing the sampling\nperiod changes the physical duration of a delay specified in steps.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> math<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> axis(Ts<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.005<\/span>, ff<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>, delay<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    J,b,Kp,Kd<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">0.2<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">8.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2.4<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    q<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>w<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    data<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> k <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>Ts)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        t<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>k<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>Ts<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        s<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>(t<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        r<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>s<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>s<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>s<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        vr<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>s<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>s<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>s)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        ar<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">12<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>s)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> t<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        m<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>J<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>ar<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>b<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>vr <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> ff <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        j<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(data)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>delay)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        qm,wm<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(q,w) <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> delay<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> data <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> data[<span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>(j,<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(data)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)][<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>:<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        raw<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>m<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>Kp<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(r<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>qm)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>Kd<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(vr<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>wm)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        u<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,<span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>(<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,raw))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        data.append((t,r,q,w,u,raw))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"co\"># Exact solution under constant torque over one sample.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>math.exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>b<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>Ts<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>J)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        w_inf<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>u<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>b<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        q<span class=\"op\">+=<\/span>w_inf<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>Ts<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>(w<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>w_inf)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>a)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>J<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>b<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        w<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>w_inf<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>(w<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>w_inf)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>a<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> data<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> stats(data):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(x[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> data),<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(data[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>][<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>data[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>][<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]),<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(x[<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> data),<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(x[<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>]) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> data)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> Ts <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"fl\">.005<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.0025<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-30\"><a href=\"#cb1-30\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> ff <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"va\">False<\/span>,<span class=\"va\">True<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-31\"><a href=\"#cb1-31\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        d<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>axis(Ts,ff)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-32\"><a href=\"#cb1-32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(row[<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> row <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> d)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-33\"><a href=\"#cb1-33\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(Ts,ff,stats(d))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_045_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Cubic reference trajectory, angular error of the axis with and without feedforward, and the limits of plus and minus 0.01 rad.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The simulated axis angle follows a cubic reference\nmovement; feedforward reduces tracking lag relative to feedback alone in\nthe model shown.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Results and Assessment<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-15\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Sampling period<\/th>\n<th>Feedforward<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Max. angular error (rad)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Max. velocity (rad/s)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Max. torque (Nm)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5 ms<\/td>\n<td>no<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.032146<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.93532<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.35808<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5 ms<\/td>\n<td>yes<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.000150<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.90006<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.36000<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.5 ms<\/td>\n<td>no<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.032130<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.93527<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.35712<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.5 ms<\/td>\n<td>yes<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.000075<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.90003<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.36000<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The assessment covers the motion up to 2 s and a holding phase up to\n3 s. For the idealised teaching model, we specify acceptable model\nlimits of 0.01 rad maximum angular error, 1 rad/s actual velocity and 2\nNm torque. With feedforward, all three targets are met at the simulated\nsampling instants; without feedforward, the error target is missed. When\nthe sampling step is halved, the residual error with feedforward falls\nto about half its original value. Mechanical state integration is exact\nwithin each step, but the digital control input remains constant between\nsamples. Halving the step is no substitute for a global convergence or\nstability analysis.<\/p>\n<p>A real robot joint may exhibit backlash, elastic deformation, dry\nfriction, gravity and mechanical coupling with other axes. Torque\nfeedforward would then need to be derived from a complete multibody\nmodel. A target of 0.01 rad corresponds to approximately 4 mm of\ntangential position error for a 0.4 m lever arm at small angles; an\nactual Cartesian tolerance would have to be determined from the entire\njoint configuration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What minimum duration results from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=1.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrad and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_\\max=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrad/s?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\ge3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns from the velocity limit. The acceleration limit must also be\nchecked.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What ideal feedforward torque does an axis require at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot q_r=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrad/s and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ddot q_r=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><msub><mover><mi>q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.09<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\dot q_r=0.09<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nNm.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is feedforward without feedback problematic when the model is\ninaccurate?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> Incorrect mass or friction produces a motion\nerror that pure feedforward does not correct using measurements.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Industrial\ncommunication and system integration<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://modernrobotics.northwestern.edu/chapters/chapter9/\">Lynch\nand Park – Modern Robotics, Chapter 9: Trajectory Generation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nChapter 9: trajectory generation; video index, videos not reviewed. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://modernrobotics.northwestern.edu/chapters/chapter11/\">Lynch/Park\n– Modern Robotics, Chapter 11: Robot Control<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section overview\n11.3–11.4: motion control with velocity and torque inputs; videos not\nreviewed. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M13-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M13-L5 – Industrial Communication and System Integration<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M13 – Bachelor: Automation, Control and Robotics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Sampling and\ndigital filters<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Sequential\ncontrol<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Axis\ncontrol<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Exchange measurement data,\ncontrol inputs and command information through suitable interfaces,\ndistinguish latency from data age and assess an integrated robot-axis\nsimulation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Communication as Part of the Dynamics<\/h2>\n<p>This elective focuses on timing in electrical measurement and\nactuation chains. A digital process word encodes a measurement or\ncommand; the models below require only sampling time, payload size,\ntimestamps and validity. A separate modulation or channel coding course\nis not required.<\/p>\n<p>A communication system connects the sensor, controller, actuator and\nsupervisory control system. Transmitting process values is not merely a\nquestion of bit rate: the timing and validity of a value affect the\ncontrol loop. Here, the time between acquisition and processing is\ncalled data age\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=t_\\mathrm{use}-t_\\mathrm{capture}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(with a common time reference). Transmission latency is only one\ncomponent of this age. Variations in delay are known as jitter; a\nrecurring data stream can have high bandwidth and yet provide inadequate\ntiming guarantees.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Data type<\/th>\n<th>Example<\/th>\n<th>Design requirements<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cyclic process data<\/td>\n<td>Position, rotational speed<\/td>\n<td>Known update period, validity and data age<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Events<\/td>\n<td>Fault report, target reached<\/td>\n<td>Ordering, acknowledgement or recovery<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Command data<\/td>\n<td>Target, motion duration, command ID<\/td>\n<td>Unambiguous association, no duplicate execution<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diagnostics<\/td>\n<td>History, temperature, statistics<\/td>\n<td>Analysis without blocking the axis cycle<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Cyclic and event-driven describe the temporal organisation, not\nnecessarily the network used. Publish/subscribe describes a\ncommunication relationship: a published data set can be consumed by\nmultiple recipients without a direct request for each individual\nmessage. OPC UA PubSub, for example, is described in Part 14 of the OPC\nUA specification. Real-time capability, however, does not follow from\nthe name of a protocol alone; transport, topology, scheduling and end\nsystems must meet the required bounds.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Interface Contract in the Teaching System<\/h2>\n<p>The axis from M13-L4 receives a command from the sequential\ncontroller containing <code>command_id<\/code>, <code>q_target_rad<\/code>\nand <code>duration_s<\/code>. The timing model of the axis generates the\nreference trajectory locally. The sensor supplies <code>q_rad<\/code>,\n<code>omega_rad_s<\/code>, <code>sample_time_s<\/code>, <code>seq<\/code>\nand <code>valid<\/code>. The controller output is <code>torque_Nm<\/code>;\na status message contains <code>command_id<\/code>, <code>state<\/code>,\n<code>in_position<\/code> and <code>fault_code<\/code>. Clear units\nprevent confusion between degrees and radians, for example. The receiver\nchecks the value range, time reference, sequential numbering (accounting\nfor possible wraparound) and validity; a valid but stale measurement is\ninadequate.<\/p>\n<p>For the teaching model, we use the following assumptions: axis cycle\n5 ms, sequential control cycle 20 ms, target position 1.2 rad in 2 s.\nThe assumed freshness limit for the axis controller is 10 ms. A\ntarget-reached indication is valid only after 2 s of motion and when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|q-Q|\\le0.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrad and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\omega|\\le0.02<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrad/s. If it is still absent after 3 s, the sequential controller\nsignals a timeout. In the model, invalid or stale data cause a fault to\nbe reported and block a new motion command. How a real axis is safely\nbrought to a stop following communication loss must not be inferred from\nthis abstract state transition.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Delay Experiment and System Test<\/h2>\n<p>We deliberately allow the controller to use stale measurement data.\nThis is <strong>fault injection without effective freshness\nmonitoring<\/strong>, intended to show why such monitoring is necessary.\nThe timestamp of the reference trajectory remains current; only angle\nand velocity measurements are delayed. A delay of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsteps corresponds to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dT_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe use the same ideal axis as in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L4<\/a>:\nno new mechanical parameterisation, no packet loss and no random\njitter.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> math<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> axis(Ts<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.005<\/span>, ff<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>, delay<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    J,b,Kp,Kd<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">0.2<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">8.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2.4<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    q<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>w<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    data<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> k <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>Ts)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        t<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>k<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>Ts<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        s<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>(t<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        r<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>s<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>s<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>s<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        vr<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>s<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>s<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>s)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        ar<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">12<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>s)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> t<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        m<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>J<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>ar<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>b<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>vr <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> ff <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        j<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(data)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>delay)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        qm,wm<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(q,w) <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> delay<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> data <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> data[<span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>(j,<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(data)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)][<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>:<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        raw<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>m<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>Kp<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(r<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>qm)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>Kd<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(vr<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>wm)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        u<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,<span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>(<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,raw))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        data.append((t,r,q,w,u,raw))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"co\"># Exact solution under constant torque over one sample.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>math.exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>b<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>Ts<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>J)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        w_inf<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>u<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>b<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        q<span class=\"op\">+=<\/span>w_inf<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>Ts<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>(w<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>w_inf)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>a)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>J<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>b<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        w<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>w_inf<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>(w<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>w_inf)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>a<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> data<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> stats(data):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(x[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> data),<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(data[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>][<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>data[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>][<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]),<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(x[<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> data),<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(x[<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>]) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> data)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> D <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">60<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-30\"><a href=\"#cb1-30\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    d<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>axis(delay<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>D)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-31\"><a href=\"#cb1-31\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(D,stats(d))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-32\"><a href=\"#cb1-32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    end<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>d[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-33\"><a href=\"#cb1-33\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;Ziel am Ende:&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(end[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.01<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(end[<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.02<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-34\"><a href=\"#cb1-34\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-35\"><a href=\"#cb1-35\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> freshness(now, stamp, valid):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-36\"><a href=\"#cb1-36\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> valid <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> now<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>stamp <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.010<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-12<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-37\"><a href=\"#cb1-37\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-38\"><a href=\"#cb1-38\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> freshness(<span class=\"fl\">1.010<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"va\">True<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-39\"><a href=\"#cb1-39\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> freshness(<span class=\"fl\">1.020<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"va\">True<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-40\"><a href=\"#cb1-40\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> freshness(<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"va\">False<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-41\"><a href=\"#cb1-41\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> freshness(<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.001<\/span>,<span class=\"va\">True<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_046_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Simulated angular errors for four fixed measurement delays, with a detail panel showing the smaller deviations.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: As measurement age increases, the simulated angular\nerror changes; the inset also reveals smaller deviations between delay\ncases.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-15\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Measurement delay<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Max. angular error (rad)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Max. velocity (rad/s)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Max. torque (Nm)<\/th>\n<th>Error target 0.01 rad<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0 ms<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.000150<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.90006<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.36000<\/td>\n<td>met<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20 ms<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.018813<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.90082<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.44480<\/td>\n<td>missed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">100 ms<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.104466<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.04054<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.82793<\/td>\n<td>missed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">300 ms<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.429460<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4.71180<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.00000<\/td>\n<td>missed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>All four variants use the same 2 Nm limit. At 300 ms, the deviation\ngrows so much during the observation window that the control input limit\nis reached. This demonstrates unacceptable behaviour in the scenario\nunder investigation; it is not a mathematical proof of asymptotic\ninstability. Even a 20 ms delay is sufficient for the ideal model to\nmiss the path error target. With active freshness checks, measurements\ndelayed by 20, 100 or 300 ms would not be treated as valid data. Whether\n10 ms is a sufficient limit for all disturbances remains to be\ninvestigated separately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Integration, Timing Budget and Acceptance<\/h2>\n<p>For an axis cycle of 5 ms, suppose the timing budget allows at most\n1.0 ms for measurement acquisition, 0.7 ms for transmission, 0.8 ms for\ncomputation and 0.5 ms for delivery of the control input. This totals\n3.0 ms, leaving 2.0 ms of margin within the cycle. Such a sum is an\nupper bound only if the individual values are genuine worst-case bounds\nand there are no additional waiting times, queueing delays or clock\nerrors. With independently clocked systems, an additional waiting time\nof up to one cycle may occur. The freshness limit is therefore no\nsubstitute for timing analysis.<\/p>\n<p>The module project consists of three interconnected levels of\ntesting: the discrete sequential controller (M13-L1), the locally\ncontrolled axis (M13-L4) and the validated measurement data contract.\nThe preceding tests demonstrate the respective properties at component\nlevel, not the operation of an actual PLC or fieldbus. A complete\nacceptance procedure additionally needs the following integration\ntests:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Test stimulus<\/th>\n<th>Expected behaviour<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Valid start, fresh data<\/td>\n<td>MOVING (<code>FAHREN<\/code>); generate the trajectory exactly\nonce<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Position reached, rotational speed too high<\/td>\n<td>No completion indication<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Same command ID repeated<\/td>\n<td>No second command<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data age above freshness limit<\/td>\n<td>Fault indication; no completion indication based on stale\nvalues<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fault simultaneous with target being reached<\/td>\n<td>FAULT (<code>FEHLER<\/code>) takes priority<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fault resolved, new acknowledgement edge<\/td>\n<td>READY (<code>BEREIT<\/code>); new start edge required<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>A key design decision is to execute fast control loops locally\nwherever possible and transmit supervisory commands at a slower rate.\nThis reduces time-critical dependencies. The actual benefit must be\nmeasured in the particular system or demonstrated by a sound worst-case\nanalysis. An interface model with artificial delays allows testing\nbefore a physical system is built.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A measurement was generated at 0.100 s and used at 0.114 s. How old\nis it?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> 14 ms; it is not acceptable under the 10 ms\nfreshness limit of the teaching system.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A cyclic data stream contains 40 payload bytes every 5 ms. What is\nthe payload bit rate alone?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>40<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.005<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>64<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">40\\cdot8/0.005=64\\,000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbit/s. Frames, protocol headers and other network traffic are not\nincluded.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is an <code>in_position<\/code> Boolean variable insufficient as\nacceptance information?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> It needs validity, a time reference, a command\nreference and a defined basis in the angle and velocity thresholds.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;page=study-path&amp;section=S3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Study\npathway<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Model\nvalidation<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://reference.opcfoundation.org/specs/OPC-10000-14/4\">OPC\nFoundation – OPC UA Part 14: PubSub<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Part 14, §4: PubSub overview\nand middleware abstraction; partial scope. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.plcopen.org/standards/logic/iec-61131-3/status-iec-61131-3-standard/\">PLCopen\n– IEC 61131-3<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · The 4th Edition section: controller configuration\nand communication; public overview. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
